<%= tag.div(**container_attributes) do %>
  <%# Toolbar: a single row grouped by function — on the left the listing's STATE and how it
      is remembered, on the right HOW it looks. Below the breakpoint the `toolbar-overflow`
      controller MOVES the secondary controls into the ⋯ menu (see OVERFLOW_PRIORITIES) and
      brings them back on the way up. %>
  <%# Without JS there is nobody to strip the attribute that reserves the place of the
      collapsible controls, so they are uncovered here. It is the only way the row stays
      complete for whoever does not run scripts: the attribute has to come from the server
      —the flicker it prevents happens BEFORE the bundle runs— and therefore cannot depend on
      JS to go away. `!important` because the utility that hides them lives in
      @layer utilities. %>
  <% if show_toolbar? && overflow_menu? %>
    <noscript>
      <style>[data-toolbar-overflow-settling] [data-toolbar-overflow-target="item"] { visibility: visible !important }</style>
    </noscript>
  <% end %>

  <% if show_toolbar? %>
    <%= tag.div(**toolbar_attributes) do %>
      <%# The four groups align to the bottom, same as the row that contains them: the line
          of controls is what the eye uses as a reference, and the `SimpleFilters` block is
          twice as tall as its neighbours because it carries the label on top. Aligning the
          row and leaving the groups centered fixes one level and leaves the other: measured
          on /admin/studios at 2600px, "Views" and the persistence marker sat at 0 from the
          Filter button, but "Group by" and "Columns" —which live in THIS group, the only one
          that contains the tall block— sat 11px higher. With items of equal height both
          alignments give the same result, so nothing changes for the other three; they are
          written the same way so the next control taller than its neighbours does not reopen
          this.

          Left, first subgroup: the CONTENT of the view — which rows and which columns.
          `flex-1` only below `sm`: there the search has to fill the row, and above `sm` it is
          fixed width (`sm:w-64`), so by growing it pushed the separator and the views against
          the view switch instead of leaving the gap where it belongs. %>
      <% if show_toolbar_left? %>
        <%= tag.div(**overflow_group_attributes("left", css_class: "flex items-end gap-1 sm:gap-2 flex-1 sm:flex-initial min-w-0")) do %>
          <%# What is asked for is the CONTENT and not the slot: a declared control that
              decides not to paint (e.g. saved views with no store) left an empty wrapper that
              the JS moved into the ⋯, uncovering a button that opens an empty menu. %>
          <%# `sm:flex-auto` and not `flex-1` above the breakpoint: with basis 0 this item
              carries no weight when distributing the shortfall, so a tight row left it at 0px
              and the search —which measures its own width anyway— painted itself ON TOP of
              group by and columns. With the basis taken from the content it absorbs the
              shortfall by shrinking, which is what you see. %>
          <% if control_content(:filters_panel) || control_content(:simple_filters) %>
            <%= tag.div(**overflow_item_attributes(:filters, group: "left", css_class: "flex-1 sm:flex-auto min-w-0")) do %>
              <%= control_content(:filters_panel) %>
              <%= control_content(:simple_filters) %>
            <% end %>
          <% end %>

          <%# In a mode that applies no grouping the control goes INERT, not absent: the
              disabled button itself says there is no grouping there, without moving the row
              when the mode changes. %>
          <% if group_by_control? %>
            <%= tag.div(**overflow_item_attributes(:group_by, group: "left", css_class: "shrink-0")) do %>
              <%= render group_by_control %>
            <% end %>
          <% end %>

          <%# `shrink-0`: this group DOES shrink (unlike the one on the right), and without
              this the columns button gets squashed instead of letting the search absorb. %>
          <% if control_content(:column_selector) %>
            <%= tag.div(**overflow_item_attributes(:column_selector, group: "left", css_class: "shrink-0")) do %>
              <%= control_content(:column_selector) %>
            <% end %>
          <% end %>
        <% end %>
      <% end %>

      <%# Boundary between "what the view contains" and "how it is remembered". It goes BETWEEN
          the two groups and not inside one: the JS sorts a group's children by priority and a
          node with no priority counts as 0, so the separator ended up pushed to the end of the
          row already on the first desktop render. %>
      <% if toolbar_separator? %>
        <%= tag.div(**overflow_separator_attributes("left", "memory")) do %>
          <%# Our own rule and not daisyUI's `divider divider-horizontal`: that class brings
              `width: 1rem` on `.divider-horizontal.divider`, at (0,2,0) and in the same
              layer, so NO width utility brings it down —measured: with `w-px` on top the
              computed width is still 16px—. That width adds to the row's two `sm:gap-4`, and
              the boundary measured 48px box to box (23px of air on each side of the 2px rule)
              against the 16px of any other pair in the same row. `mx-0` only killed the
              `margin: 0 1rem`, which was half the problem. With no width of its own 16px are
              left on each side, the same gap that separates the other groups, and the rule
              still measures 2×24 with the same color: `bg-base-content/10` compiles to the
              same `color-mix()` daisyUI uses in `--divider-color`. %>
          <div class="w-0.5 h-6 bg-base-content/10"></div>
        <% end %>
      <% end %>

      <%# Left, second subgroup: how that content is REMEMBERED — the named saved views and
          the marker that remembers the last state. %>
      <% if show_toolbar_memory? %>
        <%= tag.div(**overflow_group_attributes("memory", css_class: "flex items-end gap-1 sm:gap-2 shrink-0")) do %>
          <% if control_content(:saved_views) %>
            <%= tag.div(**overflow_item_attributes(:saved_views, group: "memory")) do %>
              <%= control_content(:saved_views) %>
            <% end %>
          <% end %>

          <% if filter_persistence_control? %>
            <%= tag.div(**overflow_item_attributes(:filter_persistence, group: "memory")) do %>
              <%= render filter_persistence_control %>
            <% end %>
          <% end %>
        <% end %>
      <% end %>

      <%# Host buttons: arbitrary content Bali does not cover, so they are neither listing
          state nor display mode. Their OWN group and not inside the right-hand one: there the
          JS sorts by descending priority (10 against 50) and they ended up to the right OF the
          view switch, which is the only thing that can sit against the edge. %>
      <% if show_toolbar_host? %>
        <%= tag.div(**overflow_group_attributes("host", css_class: "flex items-end gap-1 sm:gap-2 shrink-0")) do %>
          <% toolbar_buttons.each do |button| %>
            <%= tag.div(**overflow_item_attributes(:toolbar_buttons, group: "host")) do %>
              <%= button %>
            <% end %>
          <% end %>
        <% end %>
      <% end %>

      <%# Right: how they look. `ml-auto` here and not `flex-1` on the left: the auto margin
          absorbs the free space AFTER grow/shrink is resolved, so on mobile —where the left
          side does grow— it is a no-op, and above `sm` it pins the switch against the edge. %>
      <% if show_toolbar_right? %>
        <%= tag.div(**overflow_group_attributes("right", css_class: "flex items-end gap-1 sm:gap-2 shrink-0 ml-auto")) do %>
          <%= tag.div(**overflow_item_attributes(:view_switch, group: "right")) do %>
            <%= control_content(:view_switch) %>
          <% end %>
        <% end %>
      <% end %>

      <%# ⋯ menu: it only exists if there is something to collapse, and it is served hidden —
          the JS uncovers it when it moves the first control inside, so no empty menu flashes.
          No `sm:hidden`: the collapse is no longer decided by the breakpoint but by how much
          width the row has (see `toolbar_overflow_controller`), and with the class the ⋯ was
          invisible at exactly the intermediate widths where it is the only way out. %>
      <% if overflow_menu? %>
        <div class="shrink-0 hidden" data-toolbar-overflow-target="overflow">
          <%# `menu: false`: in here there are no menuitems but whole widgets (other
              dropdowns, column checkboxes, the view rename form). Declared as `role="menu"`
              the screen reader enters menu mode over a form and exposes children that role
              does not allow. This is a container popover. %>
          <%= render Bali::Dropdown::Component.new(direction: :bottom, align: :end, width: :xl, menu: false) do |dropdown| %>
            <% dropdown.with_trigger(variant: :ghost, class: "btn-sm btn-square",
                                     "aria-label": overflow_menu_label) do %>
              <%= render Bali::Icon::Component.new("ellipsis", class: "w-4 h-4") %>
            <% end %>
            <%# Without daisyUI's `.menu` class the container no longer imposes display:grid
                + padding + hover on its children, so the utilities go without `!`.

                The dropdowns that land here (columns, views, group by) have ABSOLUTE
                content: nested, they position against this container and go off the
                viewport (measured: left -115px on a 375px phone, when they were also
                `.dropdown-end`). In flow they end up as stacked sections, which is the
                reasonable thing on a phone. Closed they are still `display:none`, so they
                leave no gaps.

                `max-sm:` is what scopes that decision to the phone it was taken for. When it
                was written, the ⋯ WAS the mobile mode; since #842 the valve trips at any
                width — measured, it takes four controls at 1900px. On a desktop, stacking in
                flow makes the container popover GROW when a child opens: measured on
                /admin/studios, the ⋯ panel went from 320x176 to 320x338 when Views was
                opened. Above the breakpoint the nested one floats again, which is what a
                popover inside another has to do.

                The height cap carries the SAME `max-sm:`, and that is what fixes #1080: a
                scroll container can only contain what is IN FLOW, so above the breakpoint
                —where the child is absolute— `overflow-y: auto` was not bounding it, it was
                CLIPPING it. Measured at 1440px on /admin/studios: a border of the Views
                panel peeked out and `elementFromPoint` at its center returned a `<td>`, with
                the control unusable at exactly the widths where the ⋯ is the only way out.
                Without clipping, the child paints inside the ⋯'s own stacking context
                (absolute + z-index), which is already above the content band. And there is
                no need to bound it: floating, the child contributes no height: the menu
                measures what its controls measure. %>
            <div class="toolbar-overflow-menu
                        flex flex-col items-stretch gap-2 w-full p-1
                        max-w-[calc(100vw-3rem)] max-sm:max-h-[70vh] max-sm:overflow-y-auto
                        max-sm:[&_.dropdown-content]:!static max-sm:[&_.dropdown-content]:!w-full"
                 data-toolbar-overflow-target="menu"></div>
          <% end %>
        </div>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>

  <%# Contextual selection row: immediate sibling of the toolbar, so it falls into the
      toolbar's own vertical gap when the controller hides it %>
  <%= bulk_actions if bulk_actions? %>

  <%# Custom summary slot (top position) or summary_position: :top %>
  <% if summary? || show_summary_top? %>
    <div class="mb-4 text-sm text-base-content/70">
      <%= summary? ? summary : default_summary_text %>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%# Content: the surface travels with the slot (with_table brings it, with_grid does not) %>
  <%= content_band %>

  <%# Footer: summary (left) + controls (right). It is the SAME PaginationFooter any host can
      render standalone, not an inline copy of it. %>
  <%# `.to_s` and not the bare slot: `capture` returns what the block WROTE into the buffer,
      and a Slot returned without rendering writes nothing — the nav came out empty in
      silence. %>
  <%= render pagination_footer do |footer| %>
    <% footer.with_controls { custom_pagy_nav.to_s } if custom_pagy_nav? %>
  <% end %>
<% end %>
